VALUES (
  '2026/05/26/the-peace-premium-and-the-disposition-effect',
  'behaviour', 'article',
  'The Peace Premium and the Disposition Effect',
  'When geopolitical relief rallies arrive, investors sell their winners and hold their losers. The research explains why, and what it costs.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>Relief rallies trigger a predictable psychological trap:</strong><span> the disposition effect causes investors to sell appreciated positions into positive news, locking in gains prematurely while holding underperformers in hope of recovery.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Iran MOU announced Sunday has driven TSX financials up nearly 1% and gold miners sharply higher,</strong><span> while energy names have fallen more than 3% on Hormuz reopening expectations -- creating an asymmetric emotional landscape inside most portfolios.</span></li>
<li><strong>Shefrin and Statman''s 1985 research</strong><span> identified the disposition effect as one of the most consistent and costly behavioural biases in investing, with empirical confirmation from Odean (1998) showing retail investors sell winners at roughly 50% higher rates than losers.</span></li>
<li><strong>Advisors who understand this moment</strong><span> can interrupt the disposition effect before clients act on it -- the relief rally creates a narrow prospecting window with DIY investors most exposed.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>When President Trump announced Sunday that a memorandum of understanding with Iran was "largely negotiated," global equity markets moved quickly. The TSX rose more than 1% Monday on hopes that the Strait of Hormuz would reopen, energy inflation would ease, and the worst of the geopolitical risk premium would begin to fade. It was, by every measure, a relief rally.</p>

<p>Relief rallies feel good. They also, reliably, produce one of the most expensive mistakes in retail investing.</p>

<h2>What the Disposition Effect Predicts for This Moment</h2>

<p>The disposition effect, first named by Hersh Shefrin and Meir Statman in their 1985 paper "The Disposition to Sell Winners Too Early and Ride Losers Too Long," describes a consistent pattern: investors sell positions that have appreciated and hold positions that have declined, regardless of whether selling or holding is the correct decision. The psychological architecture underneath this is dual: loss aversion, which makes realizing a loss feel disproportionately painful, and the certainty effect, which makes a guaranteed gain feel more attractive than a potentially larger uncertain gain.</p>

<p>Terry Odean''s 1998 study of 10,000 brokerage accounts confirmed the pattern empirically. Investors in his sample realized gains at a rate roughly 50% higher than they realized losses. Losing positions were held an average of 124 days; winning positions were held 104 days. The consequence was direct and measurable: the sold winners outperformed the held losers by an average of 3.4 percentage points over the subsequent year.</p>

<p>This is the trade that will happen across thousands of Canadian retail portfolios in the next several days, triggered not by analysis but by the emotional signal of relief.</p>

<h2>The Asymmetric Portfolio the Iran MOU Has Created</h2>

<p>Monday''s TSX session illustrated exactly the landscape the disposition effect thrives in. The S&P/TSX Capped Energy Index fell 3.38% as crude oil dropped on Hormuz reopening optimism, erasing months of war-premium gains from names like Canadian Natural Resources, Suncor, and Cenovus. Meanwhile, the TSX Capped Financial Index rose 0.90%, with RBC, TD, and BMO each advancing roughly 1% heading into a critical earnings week. Gold miners surged: Agnico Eagle gained 4.6%, Barrick 4.2%, Wheaton Precious Metals 5.6%, as gold held above USD $4,500/oz despite some peace-premium selling.</p>

<p>The result is a portfolio split between positions that look like clear wins -- financials, gold miners -- and positions that look like losses reversing from recent highs: energy names down sharply on the week. The disposition effect''s prediction is precise: investors will sell the financials and the gold miners that have appreciated, and hold the energy names that have declined, waiting for recovery that may not arrive on the timeline they imagine.</p>

<p>The chart above shows weekly returns across three key TSX sectors over the past 12 weeks, illustrating the divergence that has opened between energy and the rest of the index since Hormuz deal optimism intensified in mid-May.</p>

<h2>Why Loss Aversion Makes Energy the Hardest Position to Evaluate Clearly</h2>

<p>Kahneman and Tversky''s prospect theory, developed in 1979, established that losses feel approximately twice as painful as equivalent gains feel pleasurable. For a client who accumulated Canadian Natural Resources or Suncor during the war premium -- positions that have fallen 3% on Monday alone and more than 10% over the past week -- the emotional weight is significant. The reference point matters enormously: a client who bought at $45 and watched the position rise to $55 before this week''s decline to $48 does not experience an $8 gain. They experience a $7 loss from their peak reference point.</p>

<p>This is the psychological architecture that produces holding when selling would be rational. The client is not irrational. They are responding to a deeply human cognitive system that was not designed for financial markets.</p>

<h2>The Advisory Opportunity in a Relief Rally</h2>

<p>The disposition effect is most powerful when it operates unexamined. The advisor who names it -- who calls a client this week and says "here is what this kind of moment typically does to portfolio behaviour, and here is why it is worth thinking carefully before you act" -- is providing something that has a direct, measurable dollar value. The research is not ambiguous about this. Odean''s 3.4 percentage point annual cost of the disposition effect, applied to a $500,000 portfolio, is a $17,000 annual drag. Framed that way, the advisory conversation is not a soft behavioural intervention. It is a return on advice.</p>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with appreciated positions in financials and gold miners are feeling relief and the impulse to lock in gains -- the certainty effect at work. Clients with energy names that have declined this week feel the pull of "waiting it out" rather than reassessing. Neither emotional state is irrational; both can lead to costly decisions. The relief rally has also created a sense that the difficult period is over, which may prompt impulsive reallocation before the geopolitical picture is fully resolved.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Hi [Client Name], I wanted to reach out this week because the TSX has had an interesting few days -- some sectors up sharply, others down, all driven by the Iran deal news rather than by anything specific to the companies you hold. I want to flag something the research on investor behaviour is very consistent about: when markets produce this kind of mixed signal -- relief on one side, losses on the other -- the natural instinct is to sell what has gone up and hold what has gone down, and that instinct tends to be the wrong one. The positions that look good right now often continue to outperform. The positions that look like they are recovering often disappoint. Before you make any changes, let''s look at each position in the context of why you hold it, not in the context of what it has done in the last week. Can we set up fifteen minutes this week?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients who accumulated energy names during the war premium and are now watching those positions decline as deal optimism builds. These clients face the strongest pull to hold for recovery rather than reassess.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with balanced exposure to financials, energy, and materials. The portfolio split creates internal tension -- the impulse to sell winners and hold losers operates on the portfolio as a whole, not on individual positions.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients who hold gold miners and financials and have been anxious about war-premium volatility. The relief rally is genuine good news for these positions, but the conversation about whether to take profits is worth having now rather than after the impulse has acted.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review client portfolios for positions that have appreciated significantly over the past 8 weeks on war premium (gold miners, energy) and note which have reversed versus held.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients most likely to act impulsively in a relief rally: those who called during the worst of the volatility, those with concentrated sector exposure, retirees in drawdown who watch balances closely.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare a brief talking-point document explaining the disposition effect in plain language -- the Odean 3.4 percentage point cost is a useful anchor for the conversation.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document all outreach calls and the substance of advice given. The Bank of Canada''s June 10 decision and the still-unresolved nuclear elements of the Iran MOU mean this is not the end of volatility.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> A quick note on this week''s market moves<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
As you have likely seen, Canadian markets have had a notable week. The Iran deal news has driven financials and gold miners higher while energy names have pulled back on expectations that oil supply through the Strait of Hormuz will resume. This kind of mixed session -- where different parts of a portfolio move in opposite directions -- tends to trigger what behavioural finance researchers call the disposition effect: the impulse to sell what has gone up and hold what has gone down, waiting for recovery.<br><br>
The research on this is consistent: that instinct, acting unchecked, tends to reduce long-term returns. It does not mean selling is always wrong or holding is always wrong. It means decisions made in the week following a relief rally deserve more scrutiny, not less.<br><br>
I will be in touch shortly to discuss your specific positions. In the meantime, please do not hesitate to reach out.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors with energy or gold positions:</strong> The war premium built substantial gains in Canadian energy over the past three months. DIY investors who held these positions now face a confusing signal -- the conflict is easing but the positions are declining. Without an advisor, they are navigating the disposition effect alone.</p>
<p><strong>Retirees managing their own drawdown:</strong> Sequence-of-returns risk is highest for self-directed retirees. A relief rally creates the illusion that the storm has passed, which is precisely when the next decision error is most likely.</p>
<p><strong>New investors who entered the market during the conflict:</strong> Investors who started investing during the Hormuz crisis have a short reference history. Their emotional reference points are the crisis highs, making this week''s mixed signals particularly disorienting.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Hi [Prospect Name], I''m calling because this week''s market moves -- energy names pulling back while financials and gold miners pushed higher on the Iran news -- is exactly the kind of moment where people without an advisor tend to make the one mistake that is most expensive in the long run, and I thought it was worth a brief conversation.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>Relief rallies are, paradoxically, some of the highest-risk moments for self-directed investors. The emotional signal that things are improving creates the impulse to lock in gains and wait out losses -- a pattern that Odean''s research showed costs the average retail investor more than three percentage points annually. An advisor does not just provide portfolio management. They provide the behavioural circuit-breaker that interrupts the disposition effect before it acts. That is a service with a measurable, research-backed dollar value, and it is most visible in weeks exactly like this one.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How have you been managing the volatility since the Hormuz situation started in March?</p>
<p>Have you made any changes to your portfolio over the past few weeks -- added, trimmed, or moved anything?</p>
<p>When you look at your holdings right now, are there positions you are thinking about selling, and positions you are holding on to hoping they recover?</p>
<p>What would it take for you to feel confident that your portfolio is positioned correctly for the next six months, not just the next six weeks?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The mistake most investors make in a relief rally<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
Canadian markets have had an unusual week. Energy names are down sharply on Iran deal optimism, financials and gold miners are up, and most investors are looking at a portfolio with a complicated mixed message. In my experience, these moments -- when markets send conflicting signals -- are when the most expensive decisions get made.<br><br>
Behavioural finance research is quite consistent about what happens next: investors sell the positions that have gone up and hold the ones that have gone down, waiting for recovery. It feels rational. It tends not to be. The academic term for it is the disposition effect, and the average annual cost to retail investors has been documented at more than three percentage points.<br><br>
If you are managing your own portfolio and navigating this week without a sounding board, I would be glad to have a brief conversation. No obligation.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

