VALUES (
  '2026/05/26/the-hormuz-mou-and-the-capital-gains-clock',
  'tax', 'article',
  'The Hormuz MOU and the Capital Gains Clock',
  'A ceasefire framework that could reopen the strait within 60 days changes the math for clients sitting on war-premium gains in energy and gold. The planning window is narrower than it looks.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>The Iran MOU announced Sunday envisions Hormuz reopening within 60 days,</strong><span> which analysts expect will compress the war premium embedded in Canadian energy and gold mining equities -- meaning clients with appreciated positions outside registered accounts face a narrowing window to make deliberate decisions.</span></li>
<li><strong>The capital gains inclusion rate remains at two-thirds for individuals above $250,000 in annual gains,</strong><span> following the 2024 federal budget change that took effect June 25, 2024 -- a threshold that war-premium gains in energy and gold may now breach for concentrated holders.</span></li>
<li><strong>Account type matters enormously here:</strong><span> gains inside TFSAs are sheltered entirely; gains in non-registered accounts above the $250,000 threshold face the two-thirds inclusion rate; corporate investment accounts owned by CCPCs face the two-thirds rate from the first dollar.</span></li>
<li><strong>The planning conversation is not about timing the market</strong><span> -- it is about ensuring clients are not making account-type and realization decisions reactively rather than deliberately, with the June 10 Bank of Canada decision adding a second catalyst to the calendar.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>When the Strait of Hormuz closed in early March, Canadian energy and gold mining equities began a three-month repricing. WTI crude moved from roughly $65/bbl pre-conflict to above $110/bbl at the peak in mid-May before pulling back to approximately $96.60 Tuesday on Hormuz deal optimism. The TSX Capped Energy Index gained more than 40% from its pre-war baseline before this week''s reversal. Gold moved from approximately $2,900 USD/oz pre-conflict to above $4,700 USD/oz at the January record high, and is currently trading near $4,523 USD/oz.</p>

<p>For clients who hold these positions outside registered accounts -- in non-registered portfolios or inside corporate investment accounts -- the accumulated gains are substantial. The question the Iran MOU raises is not whether to sell. It is whether clients have thought deliberately about which accounts those gains sit in, what the inclusion rate implications are, and whether the planning window is as wide as it appears.</p>

<h2>The Inclusion Rate Arithmetic for Individual Clients</h2>

<p>The capital gains inclusion rate change that took effect June 25, 2024 created a two-tier structure for individuals. Annual realized capital gains below $250,000 continue to be included in income at the one-half rate. Gains above $250,000 in a single calendar year are included at the two-thirds rate. For a client who has concentrated energy or gold mining exposure in a non-registered account and who has not yet realized any gains in 2026, the $250,000 threshold may be the most important number in their near-term tax planning.</p>

<p>The arithmetic is not complicated. A client who holds $600,000 in unrealized gains across Suncor, Canadian Natural Resources, and Wheaton Precious Metals in a non-registered account, and who realizes all of it in one calendar year, faces the two-thirds inclusion rate on $350,000 of those gains. Staggering realizations across 2026 and 2027 -- to keep each year''s gains below the $250,000 individual threshold -- changes the effective tax cost materially.</p>

<p>This is not advice to sell. It is a framework for thinking about the timing of a decision that a client may already be considering emotionally in response to this week''s market moves.</p>

<h2>The CCPC Account: No Threshold Protection</h2>

<p>For clients who hold appreciated energy or gold positions inside a Canadian-controlled private corporation, the two-thirds inclusion rate applies from the first dollar of capital gains. There is no $250,000 threshold for CCPCs. This matters because a meaningful segment of high-net-worth clients -- incorporated professionals, business owners, physicians, dentists -- hold investment portfolios inside their corporations as part of a tax-deferral strategy.</p>

<p>The war premium has been generous to those portfolios. A CCPC that bought Canadian Natural Resources in January at $42 and watched it run to $58 before this week''s pullback to $49 has an unrealized gain that, if realized, faces the two-thirds inclusion rate at the corporate level. The after-tax cost of that gain is higher than the client may intuitively assume, particularly if they compare it to the personal threshold they read about in the 2024 budget coverage.</p>

<p>The chart above shows the two-year trajectory of the TSX Capped Energy Index alongside the capital gains inclusion rate timeline, illustrating where the war premium accumulated relative to the rate change and the current MOU calendar.</p>

<h2>TFSA Holders and the Different Conversation</h2>

<p>Clients who hold appreciated energy or gold positions entirely inside TFSAs face none of this arithmetic. Gains inside a TFSA are not taxable regardless of size, holding period, or frequency of trading. For these clients, the relevant planning question is different: whether the current allocation still reflects their intended risk posture, and whether the war-premium gains inside the TFSA represent a concentration that warrants rebalancing on portfolio construction grounds rather than tax grounds.</p>

<p>This is worth raising explicitly because clients often conflate the two conversations. A client who says "I am thinking about selling my energy stocks" may mean different things depending on whether those stocks are in a TFSA, a non-registered account, or a CCPC. The account type determines the entire framework of the planning discussion.</p>

<h2>The June 10 Calendar Anchor</h2>

<p>The Bank of Canada''s next rate decision falls on June 10, two weeks from today. If the Hormuz reopening proceeds as the MOU envisions and oil prices continue to ease, the BoC''s April projection -- which assumed Brent would average $90/bbl in Q2 and decline gradually -- may prove too high. Lower oil prices reduce the energy-inflation component of CPI, which changes the BoC''s calculus. A rate hold remains the overwhelming base case for June 10, but the direction of the next meaningful move shifts meaningfully if the commodity picture normalizes faster than the April MPR assumed.</p>

<p>For clients with rate-sensitive holdings -- real estate investment trusts, utilities, financials -- the June 10 decision is a second catalyst on the near-term calendar. The planning conversation that begins with capital gains on energy and gold positions does not end there.</p>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with large non-registered or CCPC energy and gold positions are feeling a mix of relief at the peace news and anxiety about what their gains are "worth" after taxes. Some will feel urgency to act before the situation clarifies further. Incorporated clients may not fully understand that their CCPC faces no threshold protection, which creates a gap between their intuitive tax sense and the actual arithmetic. TFSA holders will feel less urgency, which is appropriate, but may conflate the tax conversation with the allocation conversation.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Hi [Client Name], I am calling because the Iran MOU news this week has changed the near-term outlook for energy and gold positions, and I want to make sure we are thinking about any moves you might consider in the right context before you act. The key question I want to walk through with you is where your positions sit -- TFSA, non-registered, or inside your corporation -- because the tax picture is completely different depending on the answer. For your non-registered account, the $250,000 individual threshold on capital gains is an important number this year if you are considering realizing gains. For your corporate account, there is no threshold -- the two-thirds inclusion rate applies from the first dollar. None of this means you should sell or that you should hold. It means the decision deserves a deliberate conversation rather than a reactive one. Can we find thirty minutes this week?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated energy or gold mining positions in non-registered accounts with unrealized gains above $200,000, and all clients holding these positions inside CCPCs. The CCPC case is particularly acute because many of these clients assume they have threshold protection they do not have.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with moderate gains in non-registered accounts who are below but approaching the $250,000 threshold. Staggering realizations across years may or may not be the right strategy depending on other income sources in 2026 and 2027.</p>
<p><strong>Lower urgency:</strong> Clients whose entire energy and gold exposure sits inside TFSAs. The tax conversation is moot; the allocation conversation is worth having but is not urgent.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull up non-registered account holdings for clients with energy and gold mining positions. Identify unrealized gain size and compare to the $250,000 individual threshold for 2026.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify CCPC clients with investment portfolios holding energy or gold. Note that the two-thirds rate applies from the first dollar -- flag for immediate conversation.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Segment client list by account type: TFSA holders, non-registered holders, and CCPC holders require three different planning conversations.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Calendar the June 10 BoC decision. Rate-sensitive holdings may require a follow-up allocation review depending on the decision and the Hormuz timeline.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document all tax-related discussions with a clear disclaimer that specific tax advice should be confirmed with the client''s accountant or tax advisor.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Your energy and gold positions -- a tax planning note<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following our conversation, I wanted to put a brief note in writing on the capital gains planning considerations we discussed.<br><br>
The key points are as follows. For gains in your non-registered account, the federal capital gains inclusion rate is one-half on the first $250,000 of annual gains and two-thirds on gains above that threshold. If you are considering realizing gains this year, knowing where your total 2026 gains will land relative to that threshold is an important input to the timing decision.<br><br>
For gains inside a corporation, the two-thirds inclusion rate applies from the first dollar -- there is no individual threshold equivalent at the corporate level.<br><br>
Gains inside your TFSA remain entirely tax-sheltered regardless of size or timing.<br><br>
I will follow up to schedule time to work through the specifics for your situation. Please confirm the tax implications of any transaction with your accountant before acting.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Incorporated professionals with DIY investment portfolios:</strong> Physicians, dentists, lawyers, and engineers who hold corporate investment accounts are among the most common profiles to misunderstand the CCPC capital gains rules. The absence of a threshold at the corporate level is a gap that a well-timed conversation can close.</p>
<p><strong>High-net-worth retirees with non-registered energy or gold exposure:</strong> These clients may have held positions for years, accumulated significant unrealized gains, and are now facing a catalyst that makes realization decisions feel urgent. Without planning guidance, they may act reactively.</p>
<p><strong>DIY investors who have been active in energy and gold since March:</strong> Investors who traded into the war premium actively over the past three months may have gains across multiple positions and no framework for managing the tax implications of unwinding them.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Hi [Prospect Name], I am calling because the Iran MOU announcement this week has put a lot of investors in an unusual position -- significant gains in energy and gold, a potential reversal of the war premium on the horizon, and a capital gains inclusion rate that works very differently depending on where those gains sit. I thought it was worth a brief conversation about whether the account structure matters for you.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The capital gains inclusion rate structure introduced in 2024 is not intuitive, and the CCPC carve-out -- the absence of any threshold protection at the corporate level -- is consistently misunderstood by incorporated professionals managing their own portfolios. The difference between a deliberate, structured approach to realizing war-premium gains and a reactive response to this week''s market moves can be measured in real after-tax dollars. That is the planning value an advisor provides in a week like this, and it is concrete enough to quantify in a first conversation.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Where do your energy and gold positions sit -- registered, non-registered, or inside a corporation?</p>
<p>Have you thought about what realizing some of those gains this year would mean from a tax perspective?</p>
<p>Do you have other capital gains or losses elsewhere in your portfolio that might offset some of this?</p>
<p>Is there an accountant or tax advisor you work with who would be part of this kind of planning conversation?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The Iran deal and your capital gains -- account type matters<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
The Iran MOU announced this week suggests the war premium embedded in Canadian energy and gold equities may begin to reverse over the next 60 days as the Strait of Hormuz reopens. For investors with significant gains in these positions, the question is not just whether to hold or sell -- it is where those positions sit and what the tax implications look like in each scenario.<br><br>
The answer differs significantly depending on account type. Non-registered accounts have a $250,000 annual threshold before the two-thirds inclusion rate applies. Corporate investment accounts have no such threshold. TFSAs have no tax consequence at all.<br><br>
If you are managing these decisions without a planning framework, this is a good week to get one in place. I am happy to walk through the structure in a brief call.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

