VALUES (
  '2026/05/26/the-tsx-priced-the-mou-alone-wall-street-opens-today',
  'market', 'article',
  'The TSX Priced the MOU Alone. Wall Street Opens Today.',
  'Monday''s 1% TSX gain happened without US participation. Tuesday''s session -- the first with both markets open since the Iran framework -- will test whether the relief trade holds or overshoots.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>The TSX closed Monday at 34,830.89, up 1.04%,</strong><span> processing the Iran MOU entirely without Wall Street, which was closed for Memorial Day. Tuesday is the first session in which US and Canadian markets price the same geopolitical news simultaneously.</span></li>
<li><strong>The sector split was stark:</strong><span> TSX Capped Financials +0.90%, gold miners sharply higher led by Agnico Eagle +4.6% and Wheaton +5.6%, while the TSX Capped Energy Index fell 3.38% as WTI pulled back to $96.60 and Brent to $98.11.</span></li>
<li><strong>CAD/USD sits at 0.7236,</strong><span> weaker than Monday''s close, reflecting oil''s continued decline and the market''s reassessment of Canada''s net-exporter terms-of-trade advantage as Hormuz optimism compresses the crude premium.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Big Six Canadian banks report Q2 earnings this week</strong><span> -- BMO and Scotiabank on Wednesday, RBC, TD, and CIBC on Thursday, National Bank on Friday -- with the sector carrying a 33% index weight and having already moved 0.9% higher on Monday''s relief trade.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The TSX''s 1.04% gain on Monday was built on a structurally unusual foundation: the Iran MOU announcement hit markets on a day when the United States was closed for Memorial Day. The TSX processed the framework entirely on its own -- no S&P 500 direction, no US bond market yield signal, no Wall Street read-through. The result was a gain driven by the sectors the TSX has that New York does not: Canadian banks pricing out stagflation risk, gold miners repricing on geopolitical residue, and energy names absorbing the full weight of the crude pullback without the counterbalance of a functioning US energy futures market.</p>

<p>Tuesday changes the picture. With Wall Street reopening this morning for the first time since Friday''s close at S&P 500 7,473.47, both markets now process the same Iran framework simultaneously. The question the TSX faces today is whether Monday''s move was directionally correct and appropriately sized, or whether it ran ahead of what a fully functioning North American market would have produced.</p>

<h2>The Sector Read That Monday Delivered</h2>

<p>Monday''s TSX session produced a clean analytical signal about how the market is reading the MOU. Financials and gold miners rallied; energy sold. The logic is internally consistent. Lower oil reduces stagflation risk, which supports rate-sensitive financials by flattening the probability of a BoC hike. Gold holds because the nuclear file -- the most durable source of geopolitical uncertainty -- remains unresolved. Energy falls because the war premium that built through March and April is deflating on Hormuz reopening expectations.</p>

<p>The TSX Capped Energy Index is now down more than 10% from its mid-May peak of approximately 471 points, closing Monday at 423.39. Canadian Natural Resources fell 3.6% Monday; Suncor lost 3.0%. These are not small moves. Over the past week, the energy sector has shed more than a month''s worth of war-premium accumulation in four trading sessions. The critical analytical question is whether that deflation reflects the realistic settlement scenario or the optimistic one.</p>

<p>The chart above shows the TSX Capped Energy Index and TSX Capped Financial Index on a weekly basis over the past 14 weeks, illustrating how sharply the two sectors have diverged since Hormuz deal optimism intensified in mid-May.</p>

<h2>CAD and the Terms-of-Trade Reversal</h2>

<p>The Canadian dollar has weakened to 0.7236 against the US dollar Tuesday morning, down from 0.7244 at Monday''s close. This is the more precise signal about how currency markets are reading the Iran framework''s implications for Canada specifically. Canada is a net oil exporter. The war premium that drove Brent above $110/bbl was a terms-of-trade benefit: Canadian energy revenues rose, national income transferred to the energy sector, and the CAD traded with a commodity support it would not otherwise have had.</p>

<p>As that premium deflates -- Brent has fallen from $110 to $98 in a week -- the CAD loses that support. A complete Hormuz reopening that normalizes Brent toward $80/bbl would represent a significant terms-of-trade reversal for Canada. The Bank of Canada''s April MPR projected Brent declining from $90/bbl in Q2 toward $75/bbl by mid-2027. If the MOU accelerates that trajectory into Q3 2026 rather than spreading it over five quarters, the CAD faces a more rapid adjustment than the April baseline assumed.</p>

<h2>Bank Earnings Week: The Real TSX Test</h2>

<p>The Iran MOU is not the only variable the TSX is pricing this week. The Big Six Canadian banks -- collectively representing approximately 33% of the index -- report Q2 fiscal 2026 earnings across Wednesday through Friday. BMO and Scotiabank report Wednesday. RBC, TD, and CIBC report Thursday. National Bank closes the week Friday.</p>

<p>The banks enter earnings week having already moved higher on Monday''s relief trade: RBC +0.9%, TD +1.1%, BMO +0.9% in Monday''s session. Q1 results in late February were broadly strong across the sector, with all six banks beating estimates, capital markets and wealth management driving revenue growth, and provisions for credit losses remaining contained. The Q2 question is whether the oil shock -- which ran from March through mid-May -- has produced any deterioration in credit quality, particularly in energy-sector loan books and in consumer credit among the household segment most exposed to higher gasoline costs.</p>

<p>A strong Q2 earnings sweep from the banks, combined with continued easing in oil prices, would provide the TSX with two simultaneous tailwinds that could push the composite toward or through its May 22 intraday high of 34,574.87. A miss on provisions, or a cautious guidance tone that references oil-price uncertainty, would test whether Monday''s relief trade was premature.</p>

<h2>The Wall Street Reopening Variable</h2>

<p>The S&P 500 closed Friday May 22 at 7,473.47, within 1% of its all-time high, having processed the Iran optimism narrative across last week''s sessions. The Dow set a new intraday record Friday at 50,579.70. US markets enter Tuesday with a positive posture already established -- the MOU framework is not new information for American investors the way it functioned for the TSX on Monday.</p>

<p>The risk for the TSX is a muted US open that signals the relief trade is already priced, combined with a WTI crude price that continues lower and a CAD that softens further. In that scenario, the TSX''s energy sector faces additional selling pressure on top of Monday''s decline, the financials'' Monday gains face profit-taking, and the composite gives back a portion of the 1.04% Monday advance. That is not the base case -- but it is the scenario a disciplined advisor should understand before interpreting this week''s TSX direction as confirmation that the geopolitical resolution is complete.</p>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients are reading Monday''s 1% TSX gain as confirmation that the crisis is resolving and portfolios are recovering. Clients with energy exposure are anxious about the sector''s continued decline and may be weighing whether to sell or hold. Clients with financials and gold miners are relieved and may be inclined to take profits. The Memorial Day context -- that Monday''s move happened without US market participation -- is not something most clients will be aware of, and it is a useful clarifying detail for the advisor to introduce.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Hi [Client Name], I wanted to give you a quick update on what happened Monday and what to watch for this week. The TSX was up about 1% on the Iran deal news, which is a genuine positive signal. One thing worth knowing: US markets were closed for Memorial Day, so Monday''s move happened without Wall Street''s participation. Today is the first day both markets are open at the same time since the Iran framework was announced, which means we will get a clearer read on how the full North American market is pricing the deal. On top of that, the Canadian banks report their quarterly results Wednesday through Friday -- that is the sector carrying the most weight on the index right now. A strong bank earnings week, combined with continued oil price easing, would be a meaningful double tailwind. I will keep you updated as the week develops.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated Canadian energy positions. The sector has declined more than 10% from mid-May highs and faces further pressure if Hormuz optimism deepens or bank earnings disappoint.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients in diversified TSX-weighted portfolios. The financials gain and materials rally have partially offset the energy decline, but the net direction of this week''s session depends on variables that are not yet resolved.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with Canadian financials exposure and rate-sensitive holdings. A strong bank earnings week and continued BoC hold signal could drive further gains in the 33%-weighted sector.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Monitor Tuesday''s TSX open against the US market reopening. A muted or negative US open would signal the relief trade is priced; a positive US open would suggest momentum continues.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Track WTI and Brent through the session. The energy sector''s direction today is a direct function of crude''s response to the first full North American trading day since the MOU.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Set reminders for bank earnings: BMO and Scotiabank Wednesday pre-market; RBC, TD, CIBC Thursday pre-market; National Bank Friday. Provision levels are the key variable to watch.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note the CAD/USD level. Further CAD softening below 0.72 would signal the terms-of-trade reversal is accelerating beyond the April MPR baseline.</div>
</div>
