  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors who track the TSX daily and saw Monday''s gain:</strong> Clients managing their own portfolios will have seen the 1% move and may be drawing conclusions about the broader market direction. The Memorial Day context and the bank earnings week ahead are analytical layers a DIY investor is unlikely to have applied.</p>
<p><strong>Investors who moved to cash or reduced equity exposure in March and April:</strong> The TSX is now above the level at which many panic-sellers exited. The question of re-entry timing is directly shaped by this week''s bank earnings and the Iran framework''s next development. An advisor who can frame that decision is providing clear, immediate value.</p>
<p><strong>Recent retirees with TSX-weighted balanced portfolios:</strong> The sector rotation underway -- energy out, financials and materials in -- changes the income and growth profile of balanced portfolios that were built around an energy-heavy TSX weighting. A review conversation is warranted.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Hi [Prospect Name], I am calling because the TSX had an interesting session Monday -- up 1% on the Iran deal news -- but the context matters: US markets were closed, the banks have not yet reported earnings, and the deal itself has significant unresolved elements. I wanted to reach out to anyone navigating that picture without a clear analytical framework, because this is a week where the details matter more than the headline number.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A 1% TSX gain on a holiday Monday with no US market participation, driven by a geopolitical framework that has not yet been physically implemented, is a different analytical object than a 1% gain on a normal session with confirmed news. The advisor who makes that distinction is not being pessimistic. They are providing the kind of precision that prevents clients from making decisions based on a headline that is less conclusive than it appears. That precision is what separates an advisory relationship from a market data subscription -- and it is most visible in a week like this one.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How are you interpreting the TSX''s move this week -- does it feel like confirmation that the worst is over, or are you still uncertain?</p>
<p>Do you have energy sector exposure that has been under pressure this week, or are you more concentrated in financials and other sectors?</p>
<p>When the Canadian banks report earnings this week, do you have a sense of what you would be watching for in those results?</p>
<p>Is there a point at which you would feel comfortable re-entering equity positions you may have reduced in March or April, and do you have a framework for determining when that point has arrived?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
